  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Friday)\nIssued at 1255 PM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nA change in our long-standing dry weather pattern is finally upon\nus after a month of arid weather through the month of October. An\nupper trough along the leading edge of enhanced subtropical\nsouthwesterlies will swing across the Mississippi River Valley\ntoday and Sunday. Height falls and weak DPVA will aid in mid-\nlevel ascent while moisture increases throughout the column.\nMorning upper air observations from both LZK and OHX show that\nmoisture is abundant above 500 mb with lingering drier layers\nbelow. For now, this will lead to mainly virga over the Mid-\nSouth, but steady, southerly low-level moisture advection will\nsoon allow for rain to reach the surface this evening. So, expect\nmainly scattered rain showers this evening during Halloween trick-\nor-treating. Later in the night, low-level warm advection is\nforecast to bring 850 mb theta e values high enough to produce\n500 - 1000 J/kg MUCAPE as far north as interior portions of West\nTennessee early Sunday morning. Therefore, a few thunderstorms\nare not out of the question, mainly after Midnight Sunday and\ninto Sunday morning. MUCAPE values will decrease through Sunday\nafter the upper trough exits the region and upper level heights\nbegin to rise, warming much of the profile above 700 mb and\nleading to a steady decrease in the coverage of showers and\nthunderstorms. Forecast rainfall accumulation values currently\nsit around an inch, especially near the Tennessee River.\n\nShortwave ridging is forecast to build in over the region in\nresponse to increased low-level warm advection ahead of a new\nsystem over the Rockies early this upcoming week. Expect highs,\nwhich will remain largely in the 60s and low 70s this weekend, to\njump into the mid 70s Monday and Tuesday with dry weather. While\nthis is occurring the trough will finish phasing with the jet\nstreak within the subtropical southwesterlies located behind this\nweekend's trough, eventually ejecting out of the Rockies late\nMonday. A third trough will dive south out of Canada and into the\nnorthern Plains after this point, tugging the ejecting trough\nfurther north, leaving behind weaker, open flow across the\nsouthern Plains. This area of weaker flow will then become\npinched off and less progressive, losing amplitude as it nears\nthe Mid-South Wednesday. Additionally, the surface cyclone will\nmore closely follow the amplified portion of the upper pattern\nthrough the Midwest and Great Lakes with only a stalling cold\nfront and a nebulous area of surface low pressure over our\nregion; a trend that has only gained momentum in the last day.\nThe end result is increasing uncertainty in previously forecast\nrainfall amounts as the NBM 48 hour rainfall values for Tuesday\nthrough Wednesday are now approaching 1.5\". For now expect around\nanother inch next week with the potential for big swings towards\nmassive under- and over-performance of rainfall.\n\nThe forecast dries out through the end of the period as the upper\ntroughing lifts north and east away from the region and upper\nridging brings in mid-level subsidence. A new shortwave trough\nwill eject from the Rockies and travel towards the Mid-South late\nThursday. However, with no substantial frontal passage earlier in\nthe week, no surface temperature gradient will be present to\nproduce surface cyclogenesis. This will aid in keeping the\nforecast dry and mild towards the end of the period.",
